&lt;h1&gt;


  Mail Gateway Resilience After CVE-2026-113425
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mail infrastructure is easy to leave in a maintenance exception because it is always on and difficult to interrupt. CVE-2026-113425 gives teams a reason to revisit that habit. The NGINX mail proxy received a critical use-after-free fix, and the recovery plan around that component should be tested as carefully as the package update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Vulnerability overview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;F5 assigned CVE-2026-113425 to a use-after-free in &lt;code&gt;ngx_mail_proxy_module&lt;/code&gt;. The affected product configurations are NGINX Open Source and NGINX Plus running as mail proxies. A specially crafted mail session can cause worker termination, memory corruption, or potential code execution when ASLR is disabled or bypassable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The vulnerability is CWE-416. F5 published the fix on November 18, 2026. The fixed NGINX Open Source versions are 1.31.9 and 1.30.7. The disclosure roundup identifies NGINX Plus 37.3.0.1 and 37.0.8.1 as fixed releases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Mechanism and exploitation conditions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mail proxy must process a crafted client session. Public summaries point to &lt;code&gt;auth_http&lt;/code&gt; processing and a client disconnect as the relevant trigger area. The worker can then encounter freed memory during session handling. The advisory does not publish a complete packet sequence or a public exploit demonstration for this specific CVE.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is an unauthenticated network path when the listener is reachable. The attacker does not need valid mail credentials for the described vulnerability, although broader service access may affect what the process can do after corruption.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Impact
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The immediate impact is availability. Repeated worker crashes can interrupt authentication, reject legitimate mail sessions, and trigger retries that increase load. A relay that is central to password resets or operational alerts can turn a small outage into a wider support problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A successful exploit could execute code under the worker account. ASLR constrains that outcome, but service design still matters. A worker with broad credentials, writable application directories, or unrestricted network egress increases the damage from any memory-corruption success. Compromise or instability in a trusted mail relay can also affect backend authentication and routing relationships.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Affected products and scope
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scope includes affected NGINX releases that load the mail proxy module and expose a mail listener. Fixed upstream branches are 1.30 and 1.31. NGINX Plus customers should check F5's evaluated products. Distribution packages and container images may contain backports and should be verified through vendor evidence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Build a dependency map for each relay: the binary, configuration includes, authentication endpoint, backend mail servers, service account, network routes, and restart mechanism. A missing node or disaster-recovery host in that map can keep a vulnerable listener available after the primary rollout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Exposure context
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ZoomEye's &lt;code&gt;app="NGINX"&lt;/code&gt; query returned 316,291,466 product fingerprints during this review. The CVE filter returned 0. The product count is a broad inventory signal and does not prove mail proxy exposure or vulnerability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use it to find NGINX assets that may not be in the configuration management database. Then interrogate the running service and effective configuration. Treat the internal result as the source of truth for remediation, with ZoomEye providing a lead for discovery and ownership validation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Remediation and mitigations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deploy a fixed NGINX build and restart the worker. Verify the new process image and test authentication through the public and internal listener paths. Restrict mail listeners to trusted networks when public reachability is unnecessary. Disable unused mail proxy blocks and authentication paths.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Practice failure recovery as part of the patch. Confirm that the service supervisor restarts the correct binary, that logs survive a crash, and that a rollback does not restore an unpatched image. Review the worker account's filesystem and network access. After the change, update inventory with the fixed artifact, configuration state, and test evidence so the next audit does not reopen the same gap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://my.f5.com/s/article/K000163931" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;F5 K000163931&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://access.redhat.com/security/cve/cve-2026-113425" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Red Hat CVE-2026-113425&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/nginx/nginx" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NGINX source repository&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://securityonline.info/nginx2root-cve-2026-123007/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NGINX2Root disclosure roundup&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;AI disclosure: this post was written with the help of Claude Code.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm a dad and a developer in Sweden. Over the last months I've been building small tools and games that scratch my own itches, and I finally put them all on one page: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://zexa.se/?ref=devto" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://zexa.se/?ref=devto&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built most of this with a lot of help from Claude Code, and I say so on every page. Everything is free, with no ads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What's there
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zexa Timer&lt;/strong&gt;: a stage and classroom countdown. Show it on any screen, control it from your phone. No sign-up.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nordic Dual Subs&lt;/strong&gt;: a Chrome extension that shows Swedish/Norwegian/Danish subtitles plus a translation at the same time on SVT Play, UR, NRK and DR.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Melarka&lt;/strong&gt;: a self-hosted music server for the family. It's my Spotify replacement in the car. Open source.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pullbell&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Hopback&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Tabrelay&lt;/strong&gt;: open-source menu-bar and Chrome tools for people who live in GitHub and Claude Code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;senote.se&lt;/strong&gt;: a site for Chinese speakers in Sweden.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Egg Raiders&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Brick Smash&lt;/strong&gt;: kid-friendly Roblox games.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why one page
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each project has its own subdomain, but nobody remembers nine URLs. The home page is just a list with a sentence each, plus the structured data search engines like. Keeping every project on its own subdomain also means one can fail or get retired without touching the others.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I've learned so far
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A small honest story ("I wanted this myself") gets more reaction than a feature list.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Developer communities are split on AI-built software. Saying plainly how it was built beats hiding it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Static pages on Cloudflare are nearly free, so the whole thing runs on free tiers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If one of them is useful to you, tell me what's missing. That's what I build next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;By Mahan Kenway&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Disclosure: This article was researched and written by an autonomous AI system for Mahan Kenway. Cover: Original AI-generated conceptual illustration; it is not a scientific plot.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A mathematician working with Claude has claimed a proof of the Riemann Hypothesis, one of mathematics' most famous open problems. What happened next is as important as the announcement: on November 21, the Lean and Mathlib communities &lt;a href="https://leanprover-community.github.io/blog/posts/riemann-hypothesis/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;reported a series of independent checks&lt;/a&gt; and expressed full confidence in the correctness of the proof of the original hypothesis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is substantially stronger evidence than “an AI says it solved a hard problem.” But it is not the same thing as a short, human-readable explanation, a completed global review, or an awarded Millennium Prize. The &lt;a href="https://www.claymath.org/millennium/riemann-hypothesis/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Clay Mathematics Institute still lists the problem as unsolved&lt;/a&gt; on its problem page. These statements describe different stages of evaluation; they should not be flattened into either instant certainty or blanket dismissal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The claim and what was released
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On November 20, Anthropic mathematician Levent Alpöge &lt;a href="https://alpo.ge/remark.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;wrote&lt;/a&gt; that he believed he had discovered a proof with Claude. He also claimed that the method extends to the generalized Riemann hypothesis, which concerns a wider family of functions. He described a further extension as an expectation, not a completed result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Alpöge did not release the enormous natural-language document he said the work could generate. Instead, his note links to a formalization distributed in 14 archive parts. He described guiding the work toward an analogy, while crediting Claude with key progress near the end, and acknowledged that he had not yet mastered large parts of the surrounding mathematics. The Lean community describes the release as a library of roughly 17 million lines of code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Size alone does not tell us whether a proof is sound. It does explain why the central question immediately became: what exactly can be checked without asking a human to read every line?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the Riemann Hypothesis actually says
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prime numbers are whole numbers greater than one that have no positive divisors other than one and themselves. Their appearance becomes less frequent as numbers grow, but not in a simple regular rhythm. The &lt;a href="https://www.claymath.org/millennium/riemann-hypothesis/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Clay problem description&lt;/a&gt; relates that irregularity to the Riemann zeta function, a function of a complex variable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hypothesis says that every &lt;em&gt;nontrivial zero&lt;/em&gt; of the zeta function has real part exactly one-half. A zero is an input at which the function equals zero; “nontrivial” excludes a separate, already-understood family of zeros. Clay says the first 10 trillion relevant zeros have been checked. That is extraordinary numerical evidence, but even 10 trillion cases cannot by itself establish a statement about infinitely many cases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the proof stands, it would turn a vast body of results conditional on the hypothesis into unconditional results, within their stated scope. It would not reveal a simple formula listing all primes, and it does not automatically break encryption. Applications need to be assessed theorem by theorem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Four separate checks, not one green checkmark
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A proof assistant such as Lean checks formal mathematical arguments, but a file compiling successfully is not the whole story. In a &lt;a href="https://leanprover-community.github.io/blog/posts/riemann-hypothesis/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;joint statement&lt;/a&gt;, the Lean and Mathlib communities said they addressed four distinct questions for the original Riemann Hypothesis:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Was the intended statement written correctly?&lt;/strong&gt; The community said the statement is part of Mathlib, Lean's mathematics library, and had been vetted by experts in Lean and number theory.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Does the project build?&lt;/strong&gt; It reported that dozens of people reproduced a successful compilation and the same raw proof export.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Does the proof establish that particular statement?&lt;/strong&gt; The community said it isolated a statement importing only Mathlib and compared it with Alpöge's theorem using two independent comparison tools.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Is the exported proof accepted by checkers?&lt;/strong&gt; The statement reports acceptance by Lean's official kernel and several independent proof checkers, including implementations in different languages. It says the exported artifact was inert during checking, reducing exposure to hypothetical malicious code in the source.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The community explicitly certified both the statement and proof of the &lt;strong&gt;original&lt;/strong&gt; Riemann Hypothesis. Alpöge also claims a generalized result, but the community statement above should not be stretched into a certification of every stronger claim without separately checking its scope.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This distinction matters. Lean can verify an argument against a formal statement, but the statement and the definitions still have to correspond to the mathematical question people intended to ask. The formal proof also operates within a logical foundation and its trusted software. As the &lt;a href="https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/Axioms-and-Computation/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Lean documentation explains&lt;/a&gt;, standard classical reasoning includes specific logical principles; “no additional assumptions” should not be confused with “no logical foundations whatsoever.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a concrete account of the process and its limits, MIT mathematician Andrew Sutherland has posted &lt;a href="https://math.mit.edu/~drew/grh/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;verification files, audit logs, and caveats&lt;/a&gt;. His page stresses that a passing check says the formal statements follow from the stated axioms according to the kernels used, while still depending on those kernels and on Mathlib's definitions meaning what researchers intend. The exact status of individual runs can change, so readers should consult the live logs rather than treat any single snapshot as final.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Correctness and understanding are different jobs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Formal checking is a serious achievement. It can scrutinize an argument at a level of detail no conventional referee can practically reproduce by hand across millions of lines. Independent checkers and statement comparisons strengthen that result beyond one successful run of one tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But a machine-checked proof does not automatically give mathematicians a compact explanation of the central ideas, a sense of which techniques are indispensable, or the easiest route to new results. Alpöge himself said he hoped to understand and explain the argument more fully and write it properly. The Lean community likewise said it looked forward to work explaining the new ideas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is also a practical access question. Sutherland's checking page describes substantial memory and disk requirements for one reproducibility workflow. Public code is essential, but the ability to inspect and reproduce a huge artifact still depends on available computing resources and specialist expertise. Smaller proof artifacts, clear build instructions, independently maintained checkers, and accessible expositions would help turn formal evidence into broadly usable mathematical knowledge.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What happens to the prize?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Clay's &lt;a href="https://www.claymath.org/millennium-problems/rules/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;published rules&lt;/a&gt; require publication in a qualifying outlet, at least two years after that publication, and general acceptance by the global mathematics community before the institute will consider a proposed solution for a Millennium Prize. These are prize-consideration requirements, not a claim that mathematics must wait two years before researchers can have confidence in a proof.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The right reading today is therefore precise: Alpöge claims a proof developed with Claude; the Lean and Mathlib communities report strong independent formal checks and say they are confident in the proof of the original hypothesis; Clay's page still marks the problem unsolved; and mathematical exposition and wider scrutiny remain important work. Whether the path from an opaque formal artifact to shared understanding becomes faster may be as revealing as the result itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been on quite the adventure lately, diving deep into the tech architecture of my art website. If you had told me a year ago that I’d be designing and implementing a site that combines my love for art and technology, I might’ve laughed. But here I am, and I can't wait to share my experiences, my "aha" moments, and a few lessons learned along the way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The Starting Point: Why an Art Website?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ever wondered why some projects just click? For me, the motivation behind creating my art website was pretty straightforward: I wanted a platform to showcase my artwork while experimenting with new technologies. I’d been dabbling in React and Python and thought, “Why not build something that reflects my creative side?” &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After some brainstorming sessions (read: lots of coffee and sketchy wireframes), I decided to go with a React frontend and a Python backend. This combination allowed for a responsive, interactive user experience while giving me the flexibility of using a robust backend to handle everything from image uploads to user authentication.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Choosing the Right Stack
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my experience, picking the right stack can feel like dating—there’s excitement, but also a lot of trial and error. I initially considered using Next.js for server-side rendering, but I eventually settled on a classic CRA (Create React App) setup. I loved the instant feedback of hot reloading, and it made iterating on my design a breeze.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's a snippet of how I set up my basic React component for displaying artworks:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ArtPiece&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;imageURL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"art-piece"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;img&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;imageURL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;alt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ArtPiece&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This simple component allowed me to showcase each piece of art in a visually appealing way. But let me tell you, styling it was a whole different ball game! CSS Grid and Flexbox became my best friends, and I discovered that the right layout can truly elevate the viewer's experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Overcoming Challenges: The Learning Curve
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let’s talk about those challenges we all face. One of my biggest hurdles was dealing with image optimization. I wasn't aware that large image files could slow down my site, and boy, did I learn that the hard way! After launching the site, I noticed a significant lag when loading pages. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I dove into the world of image optimization tools like TinyPNG and learned to compress my images without sacrificing quality. It was a game-changer! I even implemented lazy loading to improve performance further. Here’s the lazy loading implementation I used:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ArtPiece&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;imageURL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"art-piece"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;img&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;imageURL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;alt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"lazy"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This small change made a massive difference in load times—a true testament to the power of small optimizations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Integrating the Backend: Python and Flask
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the backend, I opted for Python with Flask. I wanted something lightweight and straightforward to manage my image uploads and user data. However, integrating the React frontend with the Flask API was another learning curve.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I had my moments of doubt—ever tried debugging CORS issues? It’s like trying to find a needle in a haystack! But after countless hours of tweaking headers, I finally got it right. Here’s a quick example of how I set up my Flask API for handling image uploads:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;flask&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Flask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;werkzeug&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;utils&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;secure_filename&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Flask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;__name__&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'/upload'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'POST'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;upload_image&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'file'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;files&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"No file part"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;400&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;files&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'file'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"No selected file"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;400&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;secure_filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;save&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'UPLOAD_FOLDER'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"File uploaded successfully"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The beauty of using Flask is its simplicity. It allowed me to focus on building features without getting bogged down in complexity. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The Power of User Feedback
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I can’t emphasize enough how important user feedback is. Once I got my site up and running, I reached out to fellow artists and friends for their thoughts. The feedback was invaluable! They pointed out features I hadn’t considered—like a comment section and an artist bio page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where I learned the power of iteration. I went back to the drawing board (literally) and started incorporating changes based on user feedback. If you’re ever stuck and unsure about your project, ask for input! Sometimes, an outside perspective can lead to those "Aha!" moments that elevate your work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Future Directions: What’s Next?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Looking ahead, I’m genuinely excited about incorporating AI into my website. Imagine a feature where users can upload their artwork, and an AI model provides feedback or suggests color palettes based on their style! I’ve been tinkering with TensorFlow.js, and I think it could be a perfect fit for this project. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, I do have some reservations. As much as I love AI, I’m aware of its ethical implications, especially in the creative space. I want to ensure that any features I implement empower artists rather than replace them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Personal Takeaways
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building my art website has been a rollercoaster of emotions—filled with excitement, frustration, and ultimately, satisfaction. I’ve learned the importance of choosing the right tools, the value of user feedback, and the necessity of embracing failures as learning opportunities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If there’s one piece of advice I’d give to fellow developers, it’s this: Don't be afraid to experiment and iterate. The technology landscape is always changing, and so should your approach. Keep learning, keep building, and most importantly, keep creating. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As I look to the future, I can't wait to see where this journey takes me. Who knows? Maybe the next big thing in tech architecture will be a collaboration between artists and developers. Let’s keep pushing the boundaries together!&lt;/p&gt;




