&lt;p&gt;Tekken 8 runs on Unreal Engine 5. It launched January 26, 2024 for PlayStation 5, Windows, and Xbox Series X/S, built by Bandai Namco Studios with Arika and published by Bandai Namco Entertainment, and it is described as the first major fighting game to use UE5. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_8" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 engine and release&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That choice looks boring — rent Epic's shooter engine again, after Tekken 7 already used Unreal Engine 4 — until you notice what changed: Tekken 8 is the first home-first Tekken, not an arcade port, it drops PlayStation 4 and Xbox One entirely, and it rebuilds the fight around Heat aggression, Tornado extenders, Special Style one-button combos, and AI Ghosts. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_7" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 7 on UE4&lt;/a&gt; The engine did not make Tekken aggressive. The engine let Bandai fire the arcade board, the last-gen consoles, and the old hiring bottleneck at once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is for players wondering why a 3D fighter needs a blockbuster open-world engine, and for indie devs deciding whether to rent UE5 or own something smaller.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The short answer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Game:&lt;/strong&gt; Tekken 8, eighth main Tekken, tenth overall entry&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Engine:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unreal_Engine_5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Unreal Engine 5&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Before:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_7" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 7 on Unreal Engine 4&lt;/a&gt;, arcade March 18, 2015, home June 2, 2017&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Now:&lt;/strong&gt; PS5 / Windows / Xbox Series X/S only, January 26, 2024 (January 25 on Steam in some time zones)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why UE5:&lt;/strong&gt; high-fidelity characters and stages, faster content hiring, cross-play lobbies, and cinematic destruction without building a new house engine&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cost:&lt;/strong&gt; 100 GB install, no last-gen fallback, Windows minimum GTX 1050Ti and recommended RTX 2070, and a hard 60 fps law that fights against UE5's 30 fps beauty habits&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Steam lists the release as Jan 25, 2024 and describes over 32 characters, Heat, Rage Arts, Special Style, story mode, customization, Arcade Quest lobbies, Ghost AI, and replays with tips. &lt;a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/1778820/TEKKEN_8/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 on Steam&lt;/a&gt; The official site stresses the same pillars: powered by Unreal Engine 5 exclusively on PS5, Xbox Series X|S and PC Digital, with ground-up high-definition models where skin, hair and muscles flex realistically. &lt;a href="https://tekken.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Official Tekken 8 site&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The series context matters. Tekken as a franchise shipped over 61 million copies by 2025 and holds the long-running-story record without reboots. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken series history&lt;/a&gt; Tekken 7 alone sold 12 million as of July 2024. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_7" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 7 sales and UE4&lt;/a&gt; Steam now frames the series at over 59 million after the 30th anniversary in 2025. &lt;a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/1778820/TEKKEN_8/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 on Steam&lt;/a&gt; You do not rewrite that machine for fashion. You rent UE5 because you want more animators and fewer engine programmers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why UE5, not UE4 again or a house engine?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tekken 7 proved UE4 could ship a 3D fighter at scale: arcade on Namco System ES3, then PS4 / Xbox One / PC, Rage Arts, Power Crush, Akuma as guest, 12 million sold. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_7" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 7 on UE4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So why move? Three practical reasons:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Content throughput beats code ownership.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
UE4 to UE5 is not just Nanite and Lumen. It is Quixel, MetaHuman-style pipelines, World Partition habits, and a labor market where every junior already knows Epic's editor. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unreal_Engine_5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;What UE5 added&lt;/a&gt; For a roster fighter that needs 32+ launch characters plus Season 2 additions like Eddy, Lidia, Heihachi, Clive, Anna, Fahkumram, Armor King and Miary Zo, hiring speed is the engine feature. &lt;a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/1778820/TEKKEN_8/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 on Steam&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Fidelity as intimidation.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Tekken 8 rebuilt all character models and voice work instead of recycling Tekken 7 assets, added stage hazards with multi-break walls and floors, and pushed cinematic reactions to destruction. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_8" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 rebuild and Heat&lt;/a&gt; UE5's Nanite virtualized geometry and Lumen dynamic global illumination were built for film-quality photogrammetry without manual LOD pain. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unreal_Engine_5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;What UE5 added&lt;/a&gt; In a fighter, most of that detail is unreadable at 60 fps. That is the point — excess detail sells impact, not information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. No arcade anchor.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Every major Tekken before was an arcade game first, tuned for Namco boards based on PlayStation hardware. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken arcade lineage&lt;/a&gt; Tekken 8 skips arcades for home systems and adds cross-play across PS5, Windows and Xbox Series for the first time in series history. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_8" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 engine and release&lt;/a&gt; UE5's console-only target lets Bandai cut PS4 / Xbox One without maintaining two physics and art paths.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Contrast this with Capcom's opposite bet. Street Fighter 6 runs on house-owned RE Engine, launched June 2, 2023 for PS4 / PS5 / Windows / Xbox Series, later arcade and Switch 2, and passed 7 million by June 2026 with Drive Gauge and Modern controls. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Street_Fighter_6" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Street Fighter 6 on RE Engine&lt;/a&gt; Capcom uses one house engine for horror and fighting. Bandai uses Epic's rented engine for fighting. Both work, but they hire different people.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What UE5 actually changes in a round
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you only watch trailers, UE5 looks like better skin. In play, it changes pacing systems:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Heat rewards going forward.&lt;/strong&gt; When Heat is active, characters get chip damage, extra movesets, guard breaks, and dash-cancel routes. Heat timer stops when you attack. Recoverable health only returns if you hit the opponent. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_8" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 rebuild and Heat&lt;/a&gt; This is anti-turtle design: blocking without answering slowly loses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tornado replaces Screw.&lt;/strong&gt; The old Screw extender is gone. Tornado puts opponents in a ground-bound state when they fall fast, standardizing juggle routes across the taller, more breakable stages. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_8" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 rebuild and Heat&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rage stays, but re-split.&lt;/strong&gt; Rage Arts return, while Rage Drive was reworked into Heat Smash. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_8" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 rebuild and Heat&lt;/a&gt; New players get Special Style one-button combos, while veterans keep classic inputs. &lt;a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/1778820/TEKKEN_8/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 on Steam&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lobbies become content.&lt;/strong&gt; Arcade Quest lets you make an avatar and climb fictional arcades as a tutorial skin. Tekken Fight Lounge splits into Battle Area, Customization Shop, Tekken Dojo and Beach Area, with chat, emotes and matchmaking. Super Ghost Battle lets you fight AI Ghosts that learn from player data. My Replays &amp;amp; Tips pauses your replay, tells you the optimal punish, and lets you practice it on the spot. &lt;a href="https://tekken.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Official Tekken 8 site&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of this requires Nanite on paper. All of it is cheaper when your tools team is Epic's tools team.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Official system guidance makes the trade explicit: 64-bit Windows 10, i5-6600K / Ryzen 5 1600 and GTX 1050Ti minimum, i7-7700K / Ryzen 5 2600 and RTX 2070 / RX 5700 XT recommended, 8-16 GB RAM, DirectX 12, broadband, 100 GB free, with stable over 60 fps selected by default on first boot. &lt;a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/1778820/TEKKEN_8/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 on Steam&lt;/a&gt; A 3D fighter cannot dip. UE5 wants to dip for beauty. Tekken 8 spends UE5's budget on locked 60 fps, not on bigger maps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  My take: Tekken misuses UE5 on purpose, and that is smart
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most explainers say Tekken 8 uses UE5 for graphics. That is true and useless. Here is the contrarian reading, clearly labeled as analysis, not Bandai statements:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Muscles are UI, not graphics.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
No human reads bicep deformation in a 60 fps sidestep. But your brain reads weight. When Jin's shoulder flexes before a Heat Smash, you flinch a frame earlier. UE5's high-fidelity skin and muscle flex, built from the ground up per the official site, functions like a louder footstep in Counter-Strike — wasted polygons that buy readable intent. &lt;a href="https://tekken.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Official Tekken 8 site&lt;/a&gt; My view: Bandai bought UE5 as a soundboard, not a camera.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Heat is a garbage collector for cowardice.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
UE5 makes blocking look great. Great-looking defense encourages turtling, turtling kills streams. So Heat taxes passivity with chip, recoverable-health rot, and a timer that only freezes when you attack. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_8" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 rebuild and Heat&lt;/a&gt; I read Heat as memory management: if you do not use your turn, the system frees it. It is Street Fighter 6 Burnout by other means — both punish empty Drive / Heat spending with degraded blocking and chip — but Tekken frames it as aggression, not exhaustion. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Street_Fighter_6" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Street Fighter 6 on RE Engine&lt;/a&gt; Same tax, better marketing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. The real upgrade is quitting arcades, not adding Nanite.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Arcade-first development forced Tekken to design for short sessions, coin-drop readability, and Namco boards. Home-first development lets Tekken design for Ghost training, replay tips, and lounge hanging. Those are retention systems, not round systems. UE5 did not enable them technically; UE4 could do lobbies. UE5 enabled them socially by letting Bandai hire web-like live-service talent instead of arcade hardware wranglers. The engine switch is a hiring press release disguised as a graphics patch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. 60 fps is a muzzle on UE5.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Nanite wants to scale detail by draw distance and resolution with 128-triangle meshlets. Lumen wants software and hardware ray-traced bounce. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unreal_Engine_5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;What UE5 added&lt;/a&gt; Both love 30 fps showcases like early Remnant 2 or Layers of Fear. Fighting hates variable frame time more than it loves bounce light. So Tekken 8 uses UE5 against itself: tiny arenas, fixed camera distance, no open world to stream. It rents a highway engine to drive in a parking lot, because the parking lot guarantees no traffic jam. If you are an indie dev, copy that restraint: rent big tech, then shrink the problem until the big tech cannot fail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. Renting beats owning because fighters are animation companies.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Capcom can own RE Engine because Capcom ships horror, action and fighting on one renderer and amortizes R&amp;amp;D across Resident Evil, Monster Hunter and Street Fighter. Bandai Namco Studios ships too many genres on too many timelines to amortize one house fighter engine. Renting UE5, which moved from $19 subscription plus 5% royalty roots in UE4 to source-available commercial UE5, buys shared bug fixes from Fortnite's live testbed. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unreal_Engine_4" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Unreal Engine 4 licensing roots&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unreal_Engine_5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;What UE5 added&lt;/a&gt; My blunt take: Tekken animators should animate, not maintain a shader compiler. Let Epic maintain the compiler.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What indie devs should steal (and what to skip)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Steal this:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One-button onboarding that does not nerf depth.&lt;/strong&gt; Special Style lets newcomers do flashy combos with simple inputs while classic depth remains. &lt;a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/1778820/TEKKEN_8/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 on Steam&lt;/a&gt; Street Fighter 6 does the same with Modern and Dynamic controls. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Street_Fighter_6" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Street Fighter 6 on RE Engine&lt;/a&gt; Do not gate fun behind execution. Gate winning behind execution.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Ghosts as practice, not gimmick.&lt;/strong&gt; AI Ghosts that mimic real player habits plus replay tips that pause and suggest the optimal punish turn losing into homework. &lt;a href="https://tekken.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Official Tekken 8 site&lt;/a&gt; A solo indie can fake this with recorded inputs and a simple punish hint — you do not need machine learning.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Stage damage as telegraph.&lt;/strong&gt; Multi-break walls and blast floors look like spectacle, but they also mark positions. If a wall breaks twice in the same spot, players learn spacing without a tutorial. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_8" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 rebuild and Heat&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Skip this:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Do not chase Nanite for a 60 fps duel.&lt;/strong&gt; UE5's virtualized geometry shines when you import film-quality scans without manual LODs. &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unreal_Engine_5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;What UE5 added&lt;/a&gt; A 5-person team will drown in scan cleanup. Build low-poly readable silhouettes and spend the saved time on hitboxes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Do not ship 100 GB to prove seriousness.&lt;/strong&gt; Tekken can demand 100 GB because it is Tekken. &lt;a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/1778820/TEKKEN_8/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tekken 8 on Steam&lt;/a&gt; You cannot. Compress, reuse, and cut stages before you cut frames.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try the ideas in a browser before you download 100 GB
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tekken 8's lesson is that spacing, timing and not mashing matter more than shaders. If you want to feel that without installing anything, &lt;a href="https://easyhub.games/en" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;EasyHub.games&lt;/a&gt; runs instant HTML5 and Unity WebGL games with no downloads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For hit-and-run spacing, try &lt;a href="https://easyhub.games/en/games/assassin-hero-infinity-blade" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Assassin Hero&lt;/a&gt;. It is a roguelike action game about enduring hordes and refining hit-and-run skills across Egypt, medieval cities and Valhalla hills, which trains the same in-and-out rhythm Tekken punishes you for ignoring.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For precision over button mashing, try &lt;a href="https://easyhub.games/en/games/3d-block-gladiator-sword-draw" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;3D Block Gladiator: Sword Draw&lt;/a&gt;. You draw precise lines to attack, block and dodge in an arena instead of mashing, which is closer to Tekken's punish logic than most browser brawlers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For upgrade obsession, try &lt;a href="https://easyhub.games/en/games/blade-of-dimensions" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Blade of Dimensions&lt;/a&gt;. You level swords, fight endless monster waves and hunt secret blades, a tiny loop that mirrors Tekken's customization and long-tail grind without the 100 GB commitment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of these replace Tekken's 3D movement, but they let you practice the habits Tekken 8 taxes: enter, punish, leave; draw the right line, not every line; upgrade with intent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Verdict
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tekken 8 did not need Unreal Engine 5 to draw a fighter. It needed UE5 to stop building arcade boards, stop supporting last-gen, hire faster, and make aggression readable at 60 fps. Tekken 7 proved UE4 could ship Tekken. Tekken 8 proves UE5 can retrain Tekken players to attack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you love fighting games, that is why this entry feels louder even when you cannot name a new mechanic: the engine was tuned to make defense feel expensive. If you make games, copy the discipline, not the renderer — pick boring rented tech, shrink the arena until it cannot stutter, then tax the playstyle you do not want.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sources verified November 21, 2026 via direct HTTP retrieval; search demand validated via Google and Bing engine-question suggestions for Tekken 8, Street Fighter 6 and Diablo 4.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A running tracker, a delivery app or a trip recorder has one requirement that ordinary location plugins do not meet: the positions must keep coming after the user leaves the app. On Android the supported way to do that is a foreground service with a visible notification, and the rules around it have become stricter with every release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The README of &lt;code&gt;background_location_free&lt;/code&gt; is direct about why it exists. &lt;code&gt;flutter_background_geolocation&lt;/code&gt; is maintained, but Android release builds need a paid licence. &lt;code&gt;background_locator_2&lt;/code&gt; has had no release since 2023. This plugin does one job, a location foreground service, and nothing else. It is MIT licensed, needs no licence key, and does not depend on Google Play services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What it does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A foreground service of type &lt;code&gt;location&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; It shows a notification you configure: title, text, icon, channel, colour and whether it is ongoing. You can change the title and the text while the service runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;LocationManager&lt;/code&gt; providers only.&lt;/strong&gt; It uses &lt;code&gt;gps&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;network&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;passive&lt;/code&gt;, so it works on de-Googled devices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A stream of fixes.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;Stream&amp;lt;LocationFix&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; gives latitude, longitude, accuracy, altitude, speed, bearing, time, provider and a mock flag (&lt;code&gt;Location.isMock()&lt;/code&gt; on API 31 and later, &lt;code&gt;isFromMockProvider()&lt;/code&gt; before).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A native filter.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;fastestInterval&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;minDistanceMeters&lt;/code&gt; are enforced in the plugin, because &lt;code&gt;LocationManager&lt;/code&gt; treats both as hints.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A native ring buffer.&lt;/strong&gt; It keeps fixes in app storage while no Dart listener is attached. &lt;code&gt;drainBuffered()&lt;/code&gt; empties it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Headless delivery (experimental).&lt;/strong&gt; A top-level Dart callback runs in a background &lt;code&gt;FlutterEngine&lt;/code&gt; when the UI is gone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Status.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;status()&lt;/code&gt; reports the location, precise, background and notification permissions, whether location services are on, and the battery-optimisation state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Optional restart.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;restartOnBoot&lt;/code&gt; restarts tracking after a reboot or an app update. &lt;code&gt;lastKnown()&lt;/code&gt; returns the newest cached fix across providers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How to use it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add it from GitHub:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;dependencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;background_location_free&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;git&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;https://github.com/AbdeldjalilChougui/background_location_free&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;minSdk&lt;/code&gt; is 21, and you build with &lt;code&gt;compileSdk&lt;/code&gt; 34 or newer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The manifest
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The plugin's own manifest already adds &lt;code&gt;FOREGROUND_SERVICE&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION&lt;/code&gt;, the service with &lt;code&gt;android:foregroundServiceType="location"&lt;/code&gt;, and a disabled boot receiver. Your app adds the rest:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION&lt;/code&gt; are required. On Android 12 and later the user can grant approximate location only, and that still works.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;POST_NOTIFICATIONS&lt;/code&gt; is recommended on Android 13 and later. Without it the service still runs, but its notification only shows in the Task Manager, not in the drawer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION&lt;/code&gt; is needed only when the service is started or restarted while no activity is visible: after a reboot, after an app update, or when the system restarts a killed service. You do not need it when tracking starts from the UI and the service keeps running.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED&lt;/code&gt; is needed only for &lt;code&gt;restartOnBoot: true&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Permissions
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The plugin never asks for permissions. You request the runtime ones with a permissions plugin, and the README suggests &lt;code&gt;android_special_permissions&lt;/code&gt; for the battery-optimisation and background-location Settings screens. &lt;code&gt;status()&lt;/code&gt; tells you where you are, and the README has a flow chart: foreground location first, then location services, then notifications, then (only if needed) an in-app disclosure and the background permission, and finally &lt;code&gt;start()&lt;/code&gt; while an activity is visible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Dart
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight dart"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'package:background_location_free/background_location_free.dart'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Future&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;startTracking&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BackgroundLocation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;canStart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// request permissions first&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BackgroundLocation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;LocationConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;interval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;fastestInterval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;minDistanceMeters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;priority&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;LocationPriority&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;highAccuracy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;notification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NotificationConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Recording your run'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Tap to open'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;iconResource&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ic_stat_location'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;channelId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'tracking'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;channelName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Tracking'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mh"&gt;0xFF2E7D32&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;stopOnTaskRemoved&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BackgroundLocation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;locations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;listen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'${fix.lat}, ${fix.lon} ±${fix.accuracy} m via ${fix.provider}'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BackgroundLocation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;updateNotification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'3.2 km so far'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Later:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BackgroundLocation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Future&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;onAppResumed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Fixes recorded while nothing listened, oldest first.&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;missed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BackgroundLocation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;drainBuffered&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'caught up on ${missed.length} fixes'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The error codes on &lt;code&gt;BackgroundLocationException.code&lt;/code&gt; are &lt;code&gt;permission_denied&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;start_not_allowed&lt;/code&gt; (Android 12 and later refused a start from the background), &lt;code&gt;no_provider&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;invalid_config&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;not_running&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;start_timeout&lt;/code&gt;. The stream can also emit &lt;code&gt;provider_disabled&lt;/code&gt; when the user turns a provider off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Where each fix goes
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For each fix that passes the filter, the service picks one destination:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If a Dart listener is attached to &lt;code&gt;locations&lt;/code&gt;, the fix goes there.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Otherwise, if a headless callback is registered, the callback runs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Otherwise the fix goes to the ring buffer (&lt;code&gt;bufferCapacity&lt;/code&gt;, 1000 by default; the oldest fixes are dropped first).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Buffered fixes are not replayed on the stream automatically. Call &lt;code&gt;drainBuffered()&lt;/code&gt; when your UI comes back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The headless callback
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight dart"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@pragma&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'vm:entry-point'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Future&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;onBackgroundFix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;LocationFix&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Runs in a separate isolate: no access to UI state. Keep it short.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BackgroundLocation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;registerHeadlessCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;onBackgroundFix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the callback's handle does not resolve, or the callback throws, the fix goes to the ring buffer, so it is not lost. Callback handles can change between app builds, so call &lt;code&gt;registerHeadlessCallback&lt;/code&gt; on every app start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Things to know
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Android only.&lt;/strong&gt; On other platforms every call throws &lt;code&gt;MissingPluginException&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Android's rules still apply.&lt;/strong&gt; Apps targeting Android 12 or higher cannot start a foreground service from the background, apart from listed exemptions; the plugin reports this as &lt;code&gt;start_not_allowed&lt;/code&gt;. A &lt;code&gt;location&lt;/code&gt; service cannot be created in the background without &lt;code&gt;ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION&lt;/code&gt;. From Android 14, users can dismiss the notification even if it is ongoing; the service keeps running. The README links the Android documentation for each rule.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Google Play declarations.&lt;/strong&gt; Apps targeting Android 14 and later declare each foreground service type in Play Console. If you declare &lt;code&gt;ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION&lt;/code&gt;, you also fill in the location permissions declaration, with a prominent in-app disclosure, a short video and a privacy policy. Apps that start tracking only from the UI can leave that permission out and avoid the second declaration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Battery optimisation.&lt;/strong&gt; Doze and OEM battery managers can delay or kill even a foreground service. &lt;code&gt;status().batteryOptimizationExempt&lt;/code&gt; tells you whether the app is exempt. Play policy allows asking for the exemption directly only when the core function of the app is affected.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What it does not do.&lt;/strong&gt; No geofencing, no activity recognition or stop detection, no iOS, no Fused Location Provider, no permission requests, no HTTP upload, and no persistence beyond the ring buffer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One provider per session.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;highAccuracy&lt;/code&gt; uses &lt;code&gt;gps&lt;/code&gt; (with &lt;code&gt;network&lt;/code&gt; as a fallback when GPS is absent), so indoor fixes can be slow. &lt;code&gt;balanced&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;lowPower&lt;/code&gt; use &lt;code&gt;network&lt;/code&gt;, which some de-Googled ROMs lack. GPS and network fixes are not merged.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Headless mode is experimental.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;restartOnBoot&lt;/code&gt; has conditions.&lt;/strong&gt; It restarts only when tracking was running and the app holds location permission, plus the background permission on Android 10 and later. It does not survive a force stop.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What was tested.&lt;/strong&gt; The Kotlin logic (config parsing, the ring buffer, the distance and interval filter, API-level branching, routing) has JUnit tests, and the Dart API has mocked-channel tests. The example app has an emulator integration test that checks &lt;code&gt;status()&lt;/code&gt;, the error codes, that the service runs in the foreground with type &lt;code&gt;location&lt;/code&gt;, fixes injected with &lt;code&gt;adb emu geo fix&lt;/code&gt;, a mock provider, &lt;code&gt;updateNotification&lt;/code&gt;, buffering with &lt;code&gt;drainBuffered()&lt;/code&gt;, the headless callback while the UI is in the background, and &lt;code&gt;stop()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What was not tested end to end.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;restartOnBoot&lt;/code&gt; after a real reboot, a sticky restart after the process is killed, and the headless callback after the task is swiped away from Recents. Everything ran on the Android emulator (Pixel 10 image, API 37); a physical phone was not tested.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why I built it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The README states the gap in two sentences: the maintained option needs a paid licence for Android release builds, and the free one has not had a release since 2023. In the meantime Android added foreground service types, background start restrictions and new notification behaviour, and a plugin that does not follow them fails at start-up or in Play review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I did not want another large tracking framework. I wanted the smallest piece that is hard to write correctly: a &lt;code&gt;location&lt;/code&gt; foreground service that follows the current Android rules, filters fixes natively, and does not lose them when no Dart code is listening. Permissions, uploads, throttling and storage are left to the app or to other packages, and the README says so in a "What this does NOT do" section. Using &lt;code&gt;LocationManager&lt;/code&gt; only keeps the plugin free of a Play services dependency, at the cost of the limits listed above.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Get the code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The source, the manifest guide, the permissions flow chart, the example app and the tests are on GitHub: &lt;a href="https://github.com/AbdeldjalilChougui/background_location_free" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/AbdeldjalilChougui/background_location_free&lt;/a&gt;. It's MIT licensed. Issues and pull requests are welcome.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://abdeldjalilchougui.biz/blog/background-location-free" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;abdeldjalilchougui.biz&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A parent branch can match its remote exactly while its child still contains an earlier version of that parent. In a stack of GitLab merge requests, that can make the child diff show changes that belong to the parent. This showed up in a local test for &lt;a href="https://gitlab.com/gitlab-org/cli/-/work_items/8283" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitLab CLI issue #8283&lt;/a&gt;. The useful clue was the commit graph hiding behind a perfectly reasonable &lt;code&gt;git status&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Reproduce it without a GitLab account
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This creates a disposable repository and a local bare remote. The lease-protected push below goes to that local directory.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;mktemp -d&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
git init -q --bare &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"$demo/origin.git"&lt;/span&gt;
git init -q -b main &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"$demo/work"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"$demo/work"&lt;/span&gt;
git remote add origin ../origin.git
git config user.name &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Stack Demo"&lt;/span&gt;
git config user.email stack-demo@example.invalid
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;printf&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"mode=base\n"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; settings.txt
git add settings.txt
git commit -qm &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Initial setting"&lt;/span&gt;

git switch -qc parent
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;printf&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"mode=original\n"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; settings.txt
git commit -qam &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Set the default mode"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nv"&gt;old_parent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;git rev-parse HEAD&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

git switch -qc child
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;printf&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"consume mode\n"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; app.txt
git add app.txt
git commit -qm &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Read the default mode"&lt;/span&gt;
git push -qu origin main parent child

git switch -q parent
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;printf&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"mode=reviewed\n"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; settings.txt
git commit -qa --amend --no-edit
git push -q --force-with-lease origin parent
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The amend changes the parent commit ID while preserving its subject. The child still points to the old commit. Moving &lt;code&gt;origin/parent&lt;/code&gt; along with &lt;code&gt;parent&lt;/code&gt; makes Git report that the parent is up to date without changing the child at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Ask Git about the parent and child
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;git status --short --branch
git merge-base --is-ancestor parent child
git log --format=%s parent..child
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The status line is &lt;code&gt;## parent...origin/parent&lt;/code&gt;, with no ahead or behind count. The ancestry check exits with status 1. The log lists &lt;code&gt;Read the default mode&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Set the default mode&lt;/code&gt;. That second entry is the old parent commit, even though the new parent has the same subject. This is the difference between a branch agreeing with its upstream and two branches agreeing on their history.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Replay the child from the boundary you know
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this disposable repo, &lt;code&gt;old_parent&lt;/code&gt; is the exact boundary captured when the child was created. First check that it is an ancestor of the child. Then keep a reference to the pre-rebase child and replay only the commits after that boundary.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;git merge-base --is-ancestor &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"$old_parent"&lt;/span&gt; child &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;
  git branch backup/child-before-restack child &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;
  git rebase --onto parent &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"$old_parent"&lt;/span&gt; child

git merge-base --is-ancestor parent child
git log --format=%s parent..child
git show child:settings.txt
git diff --stat parent..child
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now the ancestry check succeeds. The log lists only &lt;code&gt;Read the default mode&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;settings.txt&lt;/code&gt; says &lt;code&gt;mode=reviewed&lt;/code&gt;, and the diff contains only &lt;code&gt;app.txt&lt;/code&gt;. There is no publication step in this second block. On a shared branch, fetch and inspect the child’s remote tip before rewriting history, especially if a teammate has pushed since you last looked.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CLI regression behind this example checks three outcomes with a local bare remote: a local-only sync keeps the remote child unchanged, a later regular sync publishes the rebased child, and another sync keeps the same commit IDs. That tests the result users care about: each merge request should still show its own layer after the parent changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reference: &lt;a href="https://git-scm.com/docs/git-merge-base" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Git’s merge-base documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI disclosure: This article was drafted by an AI agent. The Git example and CLI regression were run in disposable local repositories.&lt;/p&gt;

