&lt;p&gt;If you check my profile, you'll see I've participated in around &lt;strong&gt;61 hackathons&lt;/strong&gt; across Devpost, DEV.to, DevSpot, NASA and other platforms. For a long time, I tried tracking everything in Excel and regular calendars — Excel gave me dates without a visual picture, and calendars never gave me a quick color-coded view of my actual sprint load.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted a small control center for work that has a real start date, end date, and deadline, where I could open it for 2 seconds and immediately know: &lt;em&gt;what's busy, what's free, and can I realistically take on another sprint?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On October 1, 2026, I built the first version of &lt;strong&gt;Dev Cadence&lt;/strong&gt; for myself as a Chrome Side Panel extension. Here's the &lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xh8Ie_VuA_0&amp;amp;t=2260s" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;live stream review of the first version&lt;/a&gt; where the hosts broke down the design. I used it every day through October and November, writing down every rough edge I ran into.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In October, putting all my hackathons into those color-coded cells gave me a reality check: I had zero rest days and no space for other work — no way to ship a quality MVP like that. Looking at my statuses, I saw which events were still just &lt;em&gt;Interest&lt;/em&gt; or &lt;em&gt;Join&lt;/em&gt; with no early submission yet, dropped a few without pain, and finished &lt;strong&gt;every single project I committed to&lt;/strong&gt; (on my Devpost profile, my registered and submitted counts match).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The built-in analytics showed me how heavy October had been, so I adjusted my pace and expanded the extension beyond hackathons to cover personal sprints, side projects, and regular dev work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I joined a Hybrid AI hackathon in November, I added two optional AI features while keeping the extension privacy-first: your schedule stays in your browser (with manual JSON export/import), and you never have to touch AI if you prefer filling dates manually. If you do want automatic deadline extraction and sprint pace advice, you can download Chrome's ~4 GB on-device Gemini Nano model in one click so prompts stay local on your device — or let the extension fall back to cloud &lt;strong&gt;Gemini 4 Hydrogen&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why Projects Stall (and How Dev Cadence Evolved)
&lt;/h2&gt;

