  Try it for free
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/pbfjingfiblbmomjoldcnoffgojkclne?utm_source=item-share-cb" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Install Dev Cadence from the Chrome Web Store&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It takes 5 seconds to install, works out of the box with zero sign-up, and stays out of your way until you need it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Curious about how the Hybrid AI architecture works under the hood (or want to inspect/fork the code)? Everything is open source under Apache 2.0 — check out the technical breakdown in the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/vero-code/dev-cadence#readme" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub README&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How do you keep yourself from overbooking side projects and hackathons? Drop your workflow (or feedback on the extension) in the comments — I'd love to hear what works for you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fimi07p2o3fd7ez759925.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fimi07p2o3fd7ez759925.png" alt=" " width="800" height="420"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;"AI is rotting developers' brains" makes a great headline. The evidence is more interesting than that. There is no proof that using an AI assistant lowers your intelligence. There &lt;em&gt;is&lt;/em&gt; a small but growing set of randomized trials suggesting that &lt;strong&gt;how&lt;/strong&gt; you use AI decides whether you learn or just ship.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is what the best studies found, where they fall short, and eight habits (plus a tiny script) you can use to keep your skills sharp.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;In a randomized trial, &lt;strong&gt;experienced open-source developers were 19% slower with AI tools while believing they were faster&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;In another randomized trial, &lt;strong&gt;developers learning a new library with AI scored 17 points lower on a quiz&lt;/strong&gt; about code they had just written. The speed gain was not statistically significant.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The people who kept learning used AI to &lt;strong&gt;ask conceptual questions and get explanations&lt;/strong&gt;, not to hand over the whole task.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Studies so far are small and use specific tools and tasks. Treat them as warning signs, not verdicts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Study 1: METR and the perception gap
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;METR, an independent research nonprofit, ran a randomized controlled trial with 16 experienced open-source developers working on 246 real issues in repositories they knew well. Some issues allowed AI tools (mainly Cursor Pro with Claude models, in early 2025) and some did not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result: &lt;strong&gt;allowing AI increased completion time by 19%.&lt;/strong&gt; Both the developers and outside experts had predicted a speedup of roughly 20 to 40 percent, and developers still &lt;em&gt;felt&lt;/em&gt; faster after the study.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why this matters for thinking: the gap between how fast it felt and how fast it was suggests we are poor judges of our own AI-assisted work. Fluent output feels like progress.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Caveats you should know:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It was only 16 developers, on mature codebases they knew deeply. That is the case where a human already has most of the context.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The tools were the early-2025 generation. METR has since run follow-up work with newer tools and described the data as unreliable, so don't treat 19% as a fixed number.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It measured speed, not code quality or learning.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Study 2: Anthropic and skill formation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Researchers Judy Hanwen Shen and Alex Tamkin ran a randomized trial with 52 mostly junior professional engineers who had to learn a new Python library (Trio). Half had an AI assistant, half coded by hand. Everyone then took a quiz on concepts they had used minutes earlier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;(Disclosure: Anthropic, which makes Claude, ran this study, and it also published the results, which found a downside to AI assistance for learning.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The results:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;AI group&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Hand-coding group&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Average quiz score&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;about 50%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;about 67%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Task speed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;slightly faster, not statistically significant&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;baseline&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Biggest gap&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;debugging questions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is a gap of roughly 17 percentage points, close to two letter grades. The hand-coders also hit more errors themselves, which seems to have built their debugging skills.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The interesting part: how people used AI
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not everyone in the AI group did badly. The researchers found distinct usage patterns:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Pattern&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it looks like&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Learning outcome&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Delegation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;"Write this whole thing for me"&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Low&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Progressive reliance&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Starts independent, then hands over more&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Low&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Debugging loops&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pastes errors until it works&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Low&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Conceptual questions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;"Why does this library work this way?"&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Higher&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Code plus explanation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Asks for code and an explanation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Higher&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Generate, then understand&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Gets code, then asks follow-ups until it makes sense&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Higher&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The high-scoring patterns scored well above the delegating ones. The takeaway is simple: &lt;strong&gt;staying cognitively engaged is the variable that matters.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What other research adds
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Knowledge workers.&lt;/strong&gt; A Microsoft Research and Carnegie Mellon survey of 319 workers found that more confidence in AI went with less critical-thinking effort. Work shifted from producing things to verifying and integrating AI output.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Students.&lt;/strong&gt; In a high school math experiment published in PNAS, a plain chatbot improved practice scores but hurt later unassisted test scores by about 17%. A tutor-style version that gave hints instead of answers avoided the harm.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Medicine.&lt;/strong&gt; The 2026 International AI Safety Report notes a study where clinicians’ ability to detect tumours without AI dropped about 6% after three months of AI support.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pattern is consistent: &lt;strong&gt;tools that hand you the answer help you now and can cost you later.&lt;/strong&gt; Tools that make you work protect learning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  8 habits to stay sharp while using AI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Write the plan or the test first
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before you prompt, write the function signature, a rough plan, or a failing test. You are forcing yourself to understand the problem, and the AI then fills a gap you defined.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# I write this first, then ask for help with the implementation&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;test_parse_duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse_duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"1h30m"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5400&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse_duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"45s"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;45&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse_duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Ask for explanations, not just code
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use prompts that keep you in the loop.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Explain how X works and why this approach is better than Y.
Don't write the full solution. Give me the key idea and a small example.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Treat AI code like a junior's pull request
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Read every line. If you can't explain a line to a teammate, either learn it or delete it. A good rule: &lt;strong&gt;no merge without an explanation in your own words.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Debug unaided first
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Give yourself 15 minutes to form a hypothesis before asking AI. Write the hypothesis down. This is the skill the studies showed fading fastest.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Bug: checkout total is off by 1 cent
Hypothesis: float rounding in the discount step
Test: print Decimal values at each stage
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  5. Schedule AI-free reps
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Athletes train without gear. Pick one regular session a week with no assistant: a kata, a small feature, or a code review. Think of it as maintaining a muscle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  6. Use AI as a reviewer, not an author
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Write it yourself, then ask for critique.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Review this function for bugs, edge cases and readability.
List problems only. Do not rewrite the code.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  7. Learn new tools from the docs first
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you meet a new library, read the official docs and examples first, then use AI to quiz you or fill gaps. In the Anthropic study, this is the situation where delegating cost people the most.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  8. Measure yourself instead of trusting your feeling
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The METR result is a warning that your sense of speed may be wrong. Run a small experiment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Run the experiment on yourself (2 weeks)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a tiny script that times tasks with and without AI so you can compare your own numbers.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;#!/usr/bin/env python3&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"""taskclock.py: time your tasks with and without AI, then compare."""&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;csv&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;statistics&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;st&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pathlib&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Path&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="no"&gt;LOG&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;home&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;".taskclock.csv"&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ai&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;LOG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;exists&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;LOG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"a"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;newline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;csv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writerow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"task"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ai"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"seconds"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writerow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ai&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;round&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;summary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;LOG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;exists&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"No data yet. Time a task first."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;LOG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;csv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DictReader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;flag&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ai"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"no-ai"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;times&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"seconds"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ai"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;flag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;times&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"{flag:6} n={len(times):3}  median={st.median(times) / 60:.1f} min"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;__name__&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"__main__"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;len&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"summary"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nf"&gt;summary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;elif&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;len&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ai"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"no-ai"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nb"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Timing '{sys.argv[1]}' ({sys.argv[2]}). Press Enter when done..."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"usage: taskclock.py 'task name' ai|no-ai   or   taskclock.py summary"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Usage:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;python3 taskclock.py &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"add pagination"&lt;/span&gt; ai
python3 taskclock.py &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fix flaky test"&lt;/span&gt; no-ai
python3 taskclock.py summary
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;To keep the test honest:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Flip a coin&lt;/strong&gt; to decide whether each task gets AI. Do not choose based on difficulty.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Compare similar tasks.&lt;/strong&gt; A bug fix and a new feature are not comparable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Add a retention check.&lt;/strong&gt; The next day, explain the solution from memory, with no notes. If you can't, mark it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Remember it is a small sample.&lt;/strong&gt; Ten tasks will not prove anything, but it will be your data instead of a vibe.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What these studies can't tell us
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tools change quickly.&lt;/strong&gt; The METR trial used early-2025 tools, and agents are now far more capable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Samples are small.&lt;/strong&gt; 16 and 52 people are informative but not decisive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tasks are narrow.&lt;/strong&gt; One library, one lab session, or open-source issues in familiar repos is not your whole career.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Learning is not always the goal.&lt;/strong&gt; If a throwaway script saves you an hour, delegating can be a smart choice. The risk is delegating the skills you need to grow.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  For team leads
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Onboarding juniors:&lt;/strong&gt; pair them with seniors and require explanations in PRs, not just working code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Code review:&lt;/strong&gt; ask "Why this approach?" instead of "Does it pass?" for AI-assisted changes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Metrics:&lt;/strong&gt; avoid measuring AI success by lines of code or tickets closed. Track defects, review time and onboarding speed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Bottom line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI is not making developers dumber across the board. The better reading of the research is that &lt;strong&gt;delegating thinking while you are still learning has a cost, and engaged use protects you.&lt;/strong&gt; Write the plan first, ask for explanations, read the diffs, keep some AI-free reps and measure yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wrote a broader, non-developer version of this on my blog, covering thinking, memory and creativity, if you want the wider picture: &lt;a href="https://practicaldigest.com/blog/is-ai-making-us-dumber-thinking-and-creativity?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=ai-thinking" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Is AI Making Us Dumber?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Question for you:&lt;/strong&gt; have you ever measured your own speed or understanding with and without an AI assistant? What did you find?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Sources: METR, "Measuring the Impact of Early-2025 AI on Experienced Open-Source Developer Productivity" (2025); Shen and Tamkin, "How AI Impacts Skill Formation" (2026) and Anthropic's write-up "How AI assistance impacts the formation of coding skills"; Microsoft Research and Carnegie Mellon survey of knowledge workers (2025); the 2025 PNAS high school mathematics experiment; the International AI Safety Report 2026.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is Part I of the explanation, simply because there is so much to talk about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, 11 days of madness, but my competition entry, &lt;a href="https://devpost.com/software/fifth-percent" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;5%&lt;/a&gt;, is finally complete. It is just a small — although particularly important — piece of a much bigger story.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The development was an indescribable rush, yet I was in a state of flow almost the entire time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Luckily, I already had Spider Paint. Knowing what I had at my disposal, I thought I could actually build a VR application with just 11 days left before the submission deadline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At the time, I naively thought that implementing stereoscopic rendering would be enough to satisfy most of the requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The sad truth, however, is that VR is an entirely different reality.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But let's not get too far ahead of ourselves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The Globe &lt;em&gt;try load the globe!&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to speed up development, and since AI was already capable of 3D modeling through MCP, I asked it to create a globe.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first step was to implement transparent and soft materials. Now a sphere actually looked like a sphere, and with transparent oceans, I could immediately see what was on the other side of the planet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My partner and I often joke about digging all the way to China. Well, it quickly became clear that we would actually end up somewhere in the ocean.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As always, I continued developing Spider Paint following the KISS principle. I adopted Blender's keyboard shortcut philosophy, but unlike Blender, I was thinking in terms of first-person modeling, an approach I described in my article &lt;a href="https://dev.to/pengeszikra/the-eye-of-beholder-umgd-55lm"&gt;Eye of the Beholder&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I actually developed the early versions on my phone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most important feature was the &lt;code&gt;M&lt;/code&gt; key, which allows me to shoot cuboids into the air or onto existing surfaces.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And before long, I ended up with the following sight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpxbdxcrpjlishfq65gsn.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpxbdxcrpjlishfq65gsn.png" alt=" " width="800" height="452"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;... and that's when it hit me: I could create an application about space travel.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Conceptually, my 3D editor isn't built around having an object in the center of the scene and editing it. Instead, any object can become a target, and once selected, it becomes the center of attention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since I already had MCP capabilities, and despite being a huge Star Wars fan, I started wondering: what if I could actually travel between real, known stars?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I MCP-ed the 500 nearest stars into the scene, and that's how my journey through space began ... on my phone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From there, things escalated quickly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I realized how cool it would be to have a 3D editor that allowed you to travel from the edge of your desk to the most distant galaxies — all within a single scene.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The next challenge was rendering galaxies. It quickly became obvious that I couldn't add enough individual stars to represent entire galaxies, so I turned to shaders. The downside, of course, is that you can't visit individual stars within these shader-generated galaxies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then there was no stopping me. I added some well-known black holes and exoplanets, and, of course, an &lt;code&gt;Enter&lt;/code&gt;-activated search feature so you might even find your favorite celestial object.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I deliberately wanted to avoid hyperspace jumps because, in my opinion, they take away the experience of actually traveling through the universe.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So there was only one option left:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SNP Drive — SPEED NO PROBLEM.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I made the speed levels increase exponentially, allowing you to travel across the entire universe.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can explore the universe yourself by loading the &lt;strong&gt;Deep Sky&lt;/strong&gt; scene.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Controls
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;(When orbit mode locks onto a target, your movement becomes orbital.)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt; W - speed forward   R - up          tab - next target
 S - speed backward  F - down          T - target ( cursor )
 A - left            [ - left roll   esc - cancel target/orbit
 D - right           ] - right roll.   0 - orbit
                 space - stop      enter - search target
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Maximum speed:&lt;/strong&gt; there is no limit, but there's not much point in traveling faster than a few million light-years per second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you get hopelessly lost, search for the Sun and keep accelerating until you're halfway there.&lt;/p&gt;


